cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use SiMetrics, please cite the paper listed under preferred-citation."
title: "SiMetrics: A Simulated Reliability Benchmark Dataset for Predictive Maintenance in Power Distribution Systems"
abstract: >-
  As the energy transition accelerates, predictive maintenance of power distribution
  infrastructure is critical. However, developing robust strategies is hindered by extreme
  data scarcity because real-world data is inherently proprietary. Existing public benchmarks
  target transmission operations or single-component remaining useful life rather than
  utility-reported indices like SAIFI and SAIDI. Consequently, generating synthetic data
  through physical simulations is essential to systematically study grid failures and
  benchmark AI models. To bridge this gap, we introduce SiMetrics as a simulation-based
  benchmark dataset and testbed for medium-voltage distribution networks. The simulator
  couples IEEE C57.91-form Arrhenius thermal aging, three failure channels (wear-out, an
  unpreventable external floor, and contingency overload trips), alongside customer-weighted
  outage propagation per IEEE 1366. Evaluations utilize public test networks including CIGRE
  Networks and MV Oberrhein modeled in pandapower. We define four primary tasks: failure
  prognosis, remaining useful life regression, trip-cause attribution, and maintenance policy
  optimization, while establishing robust tabular baselines. Our evaluation reveals two key
  findings. First, the evaluated topology-blind tabular baselines achieve a maximum ROC-AUC of
  0.843, while the dataset contains a statistically significant spatial cascade signal, with a
  neighbor-failure lift of 2.31x, compared with 1.49x under a degree-preserving null model and
  a z-score of 8.8. Second, the highest-AUC predictor does not yield the best maintenance
  policy, motivating direct policy evaluation against operational indices. SiMetrics is
  released to enable fair and repeatable comparisons of topology-aware prediction and
  decision-making methods.
